Momo the Meerkat and the Lions’ Big Mistake

On the wide golden savanna, where the grass whispered secrets and the dust liked to dance in the wind, there lived a very small meerkat named Momo.

Now Momo was not big.

He was not strong.

He was not fierce.

But ohhhh, Momo was clever.

And Momo had one favorite hobby in all the world:

Trolling lions.

Not in a mean way.

Just in a “I know exactly how to make you look silly” sort of way.

One hot afternoon, a great lion pride lay in the shade, trying to nap.

There was King Brugo, with a mane like a storm cloud.

There was Queen Zira, who could glare so hard that birds forgot how to chirp.

And there were three younger lions, each large, lazy, and very proud of their pouncing skills.

They were all stretched out in the dust, half asleep, when suddenly—

“HEY, WHISKER-FACES!”

Every lion opened one eye.

There stood Momo.

Tiny.

Chest puffed out.

Paws on hips.

“Who said that?” growled King Brugo.

“I did,” said Momo. “Just wanted to let you know your mighty roar sounds a little like a buffalo stepping on a duck.”

The young lions sat up.

Queen Zira narrowed her eyes.

King Brugo rumbled, “Little one… you are either very brave…”

Momo grinned.

“…or very snack-sized.”

Momo gasped dramatically. “Snack-sized? Oh no. My feelings. How will I recover?”

One of the young lions lunged.

But Momo zipped away.

He darted left.

He darted right.

He skidded between rocks.

He bounced over a termite mound.

And all the while he called over his shoulder:

“Too slow!”

“Careful, your paws are bigger than your brains!”

“Ooh, almost had me! That was your best miss yet!”

The lions roared and thundered after him, kicking up dust.

Now Momo knew the savanna better than anyone.

He knew every stump, every root, every anthill, every patch of prickly grass.

And most importantly—

He knew exactly where his burrow was.

Straight ahead was Momo’s little hole in the ground.

Except there was one problem.

It was… a bit small.

Actually, if Momo was being honest, it was very small.

He had been meaning to dig it deeper for weeks.

But digging was work.

And Momo preferred cleverness over chores.

So as he sprinted toward the burrow, an idea popped into his head.

A wonderfully terrible idea.

Momo leapt into the hole with a squeak.

The lions screeched to a stop around it.

King Brugo peered down.

All he could see was darkness.

Then Momo’s voice floated up from below.

“Aww, too bad! If only my burrow weren’t so cozy and safe. Guess you big majestic hunters can’t reach me!”

One young lion snarled. “We can dig!”

Another puffed out his chest. “Yes! We’ll dig him out!”

Queen Zira said, “That meerkat is laughing at us.”

And indeed, from underground came the faint sound of:

“Maybe use your claws, your paws seem decorative so far!”

That did it.

The lions exploded into action.

They dug with both front paws.

They clawed and scraped and shoved dirt behind them.

They widened the entrance.

They deepened the tunnel.

They grunted and huffed and worked so hard that their beautiful golden fur turned brown with dust.

Inside the tunnel, Momo scooted backward, deeper and deeper, just out of reach.

Every time the lions got close, he called out:

“Oh wow, that’s much better! A little deeper on the left side!”

Or:

“Mmm, needs more headroom! Think royal thoughts and really commit!”

Or:

“Could someone smooth the walls a little? I like quality craftsmanship!”

King Brugo was panting.

One lion’s tongue was hanging out.

Another had dirt on his nose.

Queen Zira stopped and said, “Wait.”

All the lions froze.

“It sounds,” she said slowly, “as if we are…”

She looked at the enormous, freshly improved tunnel.

“…digging his house for him.”

Silence.

Then from deep underground Momo said cheerfully:

“Oh good, you’re noticing the details now.”

The lions stared into the burrow.

Then, very slowly, Momo popped his head out from a side tunnel they hadn’t even seen.

He was standing on a little ridge above them, cool as a breeze.

He looked down at the giant pit they had made.

It was magnificent.

Spacious chambers.

Deep escape routes.

Smooth sloping walls.

Excellent ventilation.

Honestly? Beautiful work.

Momo placed a paw over his heart.

“My friends,” he said, “what a generous housewarming gift.”

King Brugo blinked.

Queen Zira blinked.

The young lions blinked.

Momo bowed.

“You have saved me days of labor. Maybe weeks. I shall tell my grandchildren of your service.”

The littlest lion said, “We… we got tricked.”

“Deeply,” said Queen Zira.

“Professionally,” added Momo.

King Brugo groaned and sat down heavily in the dirt.

Momo strutted over to the edge of his newly expanded burrow and admired it like a king looking over a palace.

“Big entryway… roomy den… multiple exits… excellent lion-powered excavation…”

He nodded.

“Yes. This will do nicely.”

The lions glared.

Momo smiled.

Then he pulled a juicy beetle from behind his back, took a crunchy bite, and said:

“You know, if you come back tomorrow, I’m thinking about adding a guest room.”

The lions ROARED.

Momo vanished underground laughing.

But as they stomped away, tired and dusty and annoyed, Queen Zira gave the faintest smile.

Because though nobody liked being fooled—

there was something impressive about a tiny creature who could turn a pride of lions into a construction crew.

And from that day on, whenever the lions saw Momo standing near his burrow with that mischievous sparkle in his eyes, they would mutter:

“Don’t chase him.”

“Definitely don’t dig for him.”

“Absolutely do not help with renovations.”

And Momo would grin and call out:

“Wise choice, whisker-faces!”

Then he’d disappear into the finest burrow on the savanna—

the one the lions built themselves.

